The '''First War of the Isthmus''' was fought from 18 April 1711 to 12 January 1716 and was the first major military conflict between [[Illypnian]] and [[Fosia]]n colonial powers in the [[New World]], fought over two co-equal objectives: control of [[Sant Cristòfor]], the principal port on the [[Isthmus of Balboa]], and the reclamation of [[Free Sinchew]], a territory in the isthmian jungle between the Zong colony of [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]] that had been governed by a confederation of Sinchewese holdouts and indigenous tribes since the [[Sinchew War]]. A [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]]-led coalition comprising the commonwealth constituents of Sinchew and [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] launched a naval expeditionary force across the [[Shendan Ocean]] to seize Sant Cristòfor, after earlier plans for a land invasion through the isthmian jungle had been thwarted by the resistance of Free Sinchew and the difficult terrain. The Zong fleet held an early advantage in Shendan waters, as the majority of the Volisanian Royal Navy was deployed on the Aethiopean side of the isthmus, enabling the rapid capture of Sant Cristòfor in October 1711 and a subsequent Zong advance into the central isthmus. The [[Volisania|Volisanian]]-led coalition, which included the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]], the [[Viceroyalty of New Volisania]], the [[Viceroyalty of Tapuya]], [[Burawa]], and the [[Province of Lacashe|Lacashe]], gradually redeployed substantial forces to the Shendan theater over the following years and ultimately surrounded the Zong expeditionary force on the [[Península de la Barcelona]] and the inner city walls of Sant Cristòfor, forcing negotiations.

The war ended with the [[Treaty of Sant Cristòfor]], signed on 12 January 1716 at Sant Cristòfor, a compromise peace negotiated directly between the commanders of the Viceroyalty of Balboa's colonial army and the Zong navy, under pressure from their respective sovereigns to end a prolonged stalemate. Under the treaty, Zong withdrew from Sant Cristòfor but received the docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports that it had sought throughout the conflict. The treaty humiliated Volisania, which had mobilised vast resources to defend a port it ultimately conceded commercial access to, and contributed to the political instability that culminated in the [[Volisanian Civil War]] of 1736. Burawa, which had committed substantial naval and ground forces at the behest of its strategic alignment with Volisania, gained nothing from the war, and the alliance's value was increasingly questioned in subsequent decades. Free Sinchew, which had governed the isthmian jungle between Sinchew and Balboa for over half a century, was conquered by Zong and Sinchewese forces during the war and reunified into the Sinchew colony. The land war, fought primarily by Sinchewese and Balboan forces in the isthmian jungle, was far more devastating than the naval operations, with disease, the use of enslaved Balboans in combat, and the first deployment of new military technologies in the Lurandian theater contributing to high casualties.

== Background ==

=== The An-Ming Understanding ===

In 1605–1606, the [[Kingdom of Zong]] and the [[Sultanate of Maros]] concluded the An-Ming Understanding, a diplomatic agreement that assigned the [[Isthmus of Balboa]] as a neutral zone between Zong and Marosan spheres of influence. The agreement was intended to prevent colonial competition from escalating into conflict and to preserve the isthmus as a corridor accessible to both powers. By the late 17th century, however, the expansion of Fosian colonial settlements in [[Lurandia]] — particularly the Zong colonies of [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] — and the consolidation of Volisanian colonial authority over Balboa had made the isthmus increasingly contested. The An-Ming Understanding's framework proved inadequate to manage the growing commercial pressures, and the neutral zone was steadily eroded by Volisanian consolidation of Sant Cristòfor as a controlled port.

=== Volisanian interference in the Sinchew War ===

{{main|Sinchew War}}

During the [[Sinchew War]] (1648–1656), Volisania provided covert support to [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] rebels in [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] who opposed the Zong colonial administration. Volisanian colonial authorities in [[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]] facilitated the supply of firearms and materiel to Baiduist militias through smuggling networks operating across the isthmus. The Zong court discovered the extent of Volisanian involvement during post-war settlement negotiations and regarded the covert support as an act of aggression against the Zong colonial sphere. The grievance persisted for decades and became a central element of Zong's case for military action against Volisanian interests on the isthmus.

A faction of the Baiduist rebels, known as the Free Sinchewese Army, refused to capitulate at the end of the Sinchew War and retreated into the isthmian jungle between Sinchew and Balboa. There, they established contact with the indigenous peoples of the isthmian forests and, over the following decades, built a polity — [[Free Sinchew]] — that effectively controlled the territory between the two colonies.

=== Free Sinchew ===

{{main|Free Sinchew}}

Free Sinchew was a confederation comprising the Sinchewese holdouts of the Free Sinchewese Army and two indigenous peoples of the isthmian jungle: the [[Karawã People|Karawã]] and the [[Apyãwa People|Apyãwa]]. The territory, located in the dense tropical forest between the Zong colony of [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and the Volisanian colony of [[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]], had been effectively governed by the confederation since the 1660s. The confederation was governed by an equal council of tribal chiefs and Sinchewese military leaders, with a Council Speaker serving as the first among equals. At the time of the war, the Council Speaker was [[Tân Huat-seng]] (陳法生), a second-generation Sinchewese who had been born in the jungle and raised within the confederation.

The Karawã and Apyãwa peoples inhabited the isthmian jungle and maintained a deep cultural relationship with the forest ecosystem. Their resistance to Zong's deforestation efforts — which had begun in the years preceding the war as part of a planned land invasion — was rooted in both territorial defence and a cosmological order that regarded the forest as sacred. The Baiduist Sinchewese population of the frontier, whose religious tradition emphasised harmony with the natural order, shared this opposition to the deforestation and aligned with the indigenous tribes against the Zong colonial administration.

=== Volisania's diplomatic and commercial strategy ===

Following the Sinchew War, Volisania pursued a strategy of weakening Zong's colonial position through diplomatic and commercial means rather than direct military confrontation. The Volisanian colonial administration sought to court the Sinchewese population by offering favourable trade terms and attempting to weaken Sinchew's economic links with [[Sant Cristòfor]], positioning Volisania as an alternative commercial partner. In 1672, the Volisanian government publicly declared its support for the [[Free Sinchewese Army]], a declaration that caused a political freeze in Zong–Volisania relations.

Despite the 1672 declaration, Volisania did not immediately pursue military action against Zong. The Volisanian government, opposed to full-scale war, calculated that it was more lucrative to develop alternative trade links with Zong's neighbours. The [[Cinc Parets-Macaio galleon trade|Cinc Parets–Macaio galleon trade]], established in 1608, provided Volisania with a lucrative trans-oceanic commercial network that linked the Shendan Ocean to the western hemisphere. The galleon route made landfall at the [[Kerama]] islands in [[Yanbaru]] before proceeding to [[Cinc Parets]] in [[Dagit]], and Yanbaruan pearls were among the principal commodities traded at the Cinc Parets fair. This trade network reduced Volisania's dependence on Zong cooperation and allowed the Volisanian government to wait nearly fifty years before confronting Zong militarily.

=== Zong's land invasion plans ===

In the years preceding the war, the Zong court developed plans for a land invasion of the isthmus aimed at seizing the [[Camí Ral]] and reclaiming the territory of [[Free Sinchew]]. The plans were drafted by [[Ong Tek-bûn]] (王德文), a military planner and court official who surveyed the isthmian frontier and proposed a combined land and naval operation. The land component required the movement of troops through [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and into the isthmian jungle toward [[Sant Cristòfor]].

The thick tropical jungle between Sinchew and Balboa made the overland route impassable for conventional military columns. To clear paths for the invasion, Zong colonial authorities began large-scale deforestation in the frontier zone. The deforestation provoked sustained resistance from the local population. The Baiduist Sinchewese communities of the frontier, whose religious tradition emphasised environmental order, opposed the destruction of the forest. The [[Karawã People|Karawã]] and [[Apyãwa People|Apyãwa]] peoples, whose territory was directly affected, mounted armed resistance against the deforestation parties and the Zong troops escorting them. The confederation of Free Sinchew, which governed the territory, coordinated the resistance and effectively blocked the land invasion route.

Faced with the impossibility of advancing through the jungle against determined indigenous and Sinchewese resistance, the Zong court abandoned the land invasion plan and shifted to a naval assault strategy. The naval expedition, launched in 1711, targeted Sant Cristòfor directly from the sea, bypassing the isthmian jungle and the territory of Free Sinchew.

=== Closure of Volisanian ports ===

Throughout the late 17th century, the Volisanian colonial administration restricted [[Kingdom of Zong|Zong]] merchant access to [[Sant Cristòfor]] and other Volisanian-controlled ports. The restrictions were part of a broader Volisanian strategy to monopolise trans-isthmian trade and marginalise Fosian commercial activity in the region. Zong merchants, who required access to the isthmian corridor to reach [[Illypnian]] markets without undertaking the costly voyage around the southern tip of [[Lurandia]], were effectively excluded from the most efficient trade route connecting the [[Shendan Ocean]] to the [[Aethiopean Ocean|Aethiopean]] and [[Kendriyan Ocean|Kendriyan]] coasts.

The [[Camí Ral]] (Royal Road), the overland route between [[Sant Cristòfor]] on the Shendan coast and [[Sant Llàtzer]] on the Aethiopean coast, was the principal trans-isthmian route. Volisanian colonial authorities imposed high transit and docking fees on Zong shipping at Sant Cristòfor, which the Zong court regarded as an impediment to its commercial interests and a violation of the spirit of the An-Ming Understanding.

=== Civilian-military tension in the colonies ===

By the early 18th century, a period of discontent had developed between the civilian governments and the military establishments in the Zong colonies of [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]]. The civilian colonial administrations, led by their respective governors, sought to expand the domestic economy and develop colonial society through trade, agriculture, and infrastructure. The [[Royal Zong Navy]], by contrast, was controlled by the central state in the Zong homeland rather than by the colonial governments, and its officer corps was more concerned with combating indigenous resistance and pursuing military glory in the Zong colonial world.

The tension was particularly acute in the navy's officer corps. By the 1600s, the navy had played a diminishing role in Zong colonial expansion, as most territorial growth during the 17th century had been achieved through overland campaigns rather than naval conquest. By the end of the 17th century, the Royal Zong Navy, though still large, was primarily devoted to protecting trade routes and suppressing rebellions in port cities. Navy admirals who had grown up hearing of the great commanders of the 15th and 16th centuries — admirals who had laid siege to cities and conquered territories across the Shendan Ocean — felt that the navy's role had been reduced to policing and escort duties. The opportunity to lay siege to a major port and conquer new territory represented a chance to reclaim the navy's martial prestige, and the admirals were eager to seize it if the occasion presented itself.

=== Military buildup at Tamsui ===

In the years preceding the war, the Zong court had authorised a military buildup at the colonial port of [[Tamsui]] in [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] in preparation for a planned assault on [[Free Sinchew]]. The 1st and 2nd Armadas of the Royal Zong Navy were stationed at Tamsui as part of this buildup, and the colonial garrison had been strengthened during the [[Second Lurandian Native Wars]] and the [[Sinchew War]]. The accumulated grievances — Volisanian interference in the Sinchew War, the closure of ports to Zong commerce, the high fees at [[Sant Cristòfor]], and the existence of Free Sinchew on territory claimed by Zong — had produced a consensus within the Zong court that military action was necessary. [[Tianzheng of Zong|Emperor Tianzheng]], who had reigned since 1681, authorised the military preparations and the planned assault on Free Sinchew. The reclamation of Free Sinchew territory and the seizure of Sant Cristòfor were co-equal objectives, though the initial preparations were focused on the land campaign against Free Sinchew.

[[Breisland]] and [[Merica]], rival Illypnian powers of Volisania, provided covert support to the Zong military preparations. The two powers sold goods and weapons to Zong and shared intelligence on Volisanian naval dispositions, seeking to weaken Volisania's colonial position without entering the war as belligerents.

== Course of the war ==

=== 1711: Outbreak and fall of Sant Cristòfor ===

The immediate trigger for the war was a minor incident at [[Sant Cristòfor]] that was deliberately exaggerated by the Zong naval command. On 3 March 1711, several Zong-flagged trading ships docked at the harbour of Sant Cristòfor to refuel and take on supplies. Refueling was permitted under Volisanian colonial regulations, though commercial trade at the port remained subject to the high transit and docking fees that had long been a source of Zong grievance. The ships were engaged solely in refueling and intended to depart once resupplied.

On 18 March 1711, a report reached the Zong naval station at [[Tamsui]] in [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] alleging that the trading ships had been impressed — seized and pressed into service — by the Volisanian colonial authorities at Sant Cristòfor. The report was false; the ships had not been seized and were simply completing their refueling. The Governor of Sinchew, [[Tân Bûn-seng]] (陳文成), proposed dispatching a diplomatic negotiator to resolve the matter through peaceful channels. However, Admiral [[Lim Kiat-seng]] (林傑成), a senior officer of the Royal Zong Navy who was visiting Tamsui at the time, seized upon the report as a pretext for military action. The admiral overrode the governor's proposal, ordered the immediate deployment of a naval flotilla to Sant Cristòfor, and imposed a communications blackout on the colonial government: Tân Bûn-seng was forced to halt all information to the Zong homeland and [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] until a successful outcome could be reported.

The Zong flotilla departed Tamsui on 8 April, covering approximately 2,600 kilometres along the coast. On 17 April, Volisanian spotters at Sant Cristòfor detected the approaching fleet and warned the Balboan colonial governor, [[Jordi de Montclar]], that Zong was moving against the port. On 18 April 1711, the flotilla entered the harbour of Sant Cristòfor. The Volisanian colonial forces, interpreting the arrival of a military fleet as a confirmed attack, opened cannon fire on the Zong ships. The exchange of fire marked the beginning of the First War of the Isthmus.

The flotilla, though initially outnumbered by the Volisanian garrison, was able to secure a foothold on the [[Península de la Barcelona]], the peninsula that commanded the seaward approach to Sant Cristòfor. The capture of the Barcelona peninsula gave the Zong forces a fortified position from which to press the siege of the inner city. The 1st and 2nd Armadas of the Royal Zong Navy, which had been stationed at Tamsui as part of the military buildup for the planned assault on [[Free Sinchew]], arrived in the following weeks with fresh troops and additional ships, reinforcing the Zong position on the peninsula.

Even before official word reached the Zong homeland, the colonial administration in [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]] learned of the attack approximately two weeks after the initial engagement. The admirals of the 3rd, 8th, and 12th Armadas, stationed at Louyang, decided to send support to the Zong expeditionary force. The Louyang governor was initially hesitant to commit colonial forces without authorisation from the Zong court, reflecting the broader civilian-military tension that characterised the colonial administrations at the time. The admirals, however, acted on their own initiative, dispatching their armadas to reinforce the siege. The civilian governments of both Louyang and Sinchew had sought to prioritise domestic economic development over military adventurism, but the Royal Zong Navy, controlled by the central state, pursued its own objectives.

Approximately four weeks after the initial attack, in mid-May 1711, word of the capture of the Barcelona peninsula was dispatched to the Zong homeland, approximately 12,000 kilometres away across the [[Shendan Ocean]]. The voyage took approximately four months. [[Tianzheng of Zong|Emperor Tianzheng]], who had authorised the military preparations at Tamsui, died in June 1711 before the dispatch arrived. He was succeeded by his adult son, who took the era name [[Tianli of Zong|Tianli]] (天理, "Heavenly Principle"). In August 1711, the dispatch reached the Zong court, and Tianli learned of the Barcelona peninsula capture. The new emperor, who had been regarded as a meek replacement for his father, seized upon the news as an opportunity to prove himself a strong leader. The [[Zong Societal Assembly|Societal Assembly]], which shared the court's imperialist disposition and regarded Volisania as a strategic threat, enthusiastically endorsed the continuation and expansion of the war effort. Official approval and additional ships with troops were dispatched from Louyang and the Zong mainland, though the court remained hesitant to commit too many forces from the colonies, requesting that the land war be fought primarily with [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]]ese resources. The Zong naval command was concerned about the vulnerability of its shipping to Volisanian naval forces in other parts of the Shendan Ocean and sought to preserve its naval strength for defensive operations.

With the reinforcements from the arriving armadas, the Zong expeditionary force pressed the siege of Sant Cristòfor through the summer and early autumn of 1711. The Volisanian garrison, commanded by General [[Oriol March]], prepared for a prolonged defence, relying on the city's fortifications and the [[Camí Ral]] supply route to [[Sant Llàtzer]] on the Aethiopean coast. However, the bulk of the Volisanian Royal Navy was deployed on the Aethiopean (eastern) side of the isthmus, leaving the Shendan approaches to Sant Cristòfor inadequately defended. The garrison could not be resupplied by sea, and the overland Camí Ral route proved insufficient to sustain the defence against the concentrated Zong siege. In October 1711, the Zong forces captured Sant Cristòfor. The city's defences, though formidable, had been weakened by the prolonged blockade and the disruption of supplies. The Volisanian garrison, exhausted and depleted, was unable to prevent the Zong assault.

The fall of Sant Cristòfor caught the Volisanian coalition blindsided. The Volisanian colonial administration had not maintained a large military presence in the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]]; the majority of Volisanian colonial forces were stationed in the [[Viceroyalty of Tapuya]] and the [[Viceroyalty of New Volisania]]. The speed and scale of the Zong expedition, which had drawn on armadas and troops already positioned at Tamsui and Louyang, exceeded what the Volisanian colonial authorities in Balboa could repel with the forces available. It took the Volisanian colonial administration several months to restructure and regroup, concentrating forces at [[Sant Bart]] in preparation for a counter-offensive. When Volisania received word of the fall of Sant Cristòfor, the Volisanian government immediately requested that its ally [[Burawa]] commit forces to the war. [[Burawa]], bound to Volisania by a strategic alignment dating to the 1680s, committed naval forces under Admiral [[Xirsi Xaashi]] and ground forces under General [[Petre Dhaxol]]. [[Lacashe]], a Burawi ally, contributed additional forces.

Following the capture of Sant Cristòfor, the Zong expeditionary force advanced into the central isthmus. From October 1711 through May 1712, Zong forces secured a sizable portion of central isthmian territory, extending their control beyond the port and the Barcelona peninsula into the interior. The Zong advance was facilitated by the absence of organised Volisanian resistance in the immediate aftermath of the fall of Sant Cristòfor, as the Volisanian colonial forces had not yet been reinforced.

Concurrently, the Sinchew colonial garrison launched a land campaign against [[Free Sinchew]], the confederation that governed the isthmian jungle between [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]]. The Sinchew colonial administration, under General [[Tan Tek-hui]] (陳德輝), regarded the naval attack on Sant Cristòfor as an opportunity to resolve the Free Sinchew question. Expecting that the confederation would fold or allow the Sinchew army to pass through its territory, General Tan Tek-hui ordered the [[Sinchew Army Corps]] to advance through Free Sinchew territory. The first engagement of the land campaign occurred in August 1711, when the 1st Forward Battalion of the Sinchew Army Corps, under the command of Major [[Lîm Tek-bûn]] (林德文), encountered Free Sinchewese forces in the isthmian jungle. Contrary to Sinchewese expectations, the Free Sinchewese forces — comprising Sinchewese holdouts and [[Karawã People|Karawã]] and [[Apyãwa People|Apyãwa]] tribal warriors under the overall direction of Council Speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]] (陳法生) — attacked the advancing battalion rather than permitting its passage.

Approximately one week after the first engagement, the Free Sinchewese leadership dispatched correspondence to its Volisanian contacts, requesting military assistance. Volisania, which had declared support for the [[Free Sinchewese Army]] in 1672, granted Free Sinchew free passage for arms and began supplying heavy weaponry to the confederation. The influx of Volisanian arms transformed the character of the land campaign: the Free Sinchewese forces, already adept at guerrilla warfare in their home terrain, were now equipped to conduct sustained resistance against the Sinchew Army Corps. The early engagements of the land campaign saw the Sinchewese battalions suffer significant losses, as the Free Sinchewese forces exploited their knowledge of the jungle paths and waterways to ambush the advancing columns and disrupt Sinchewese supply lines.

In October 1711, concurrent with the fall of Sant Cristòfor, the Sinchew colonial administration sought to address the mounting losses in the land campaign by requesting that landowners near Free Sinchew territory contribute their enslaved labourers to the war effort. In exchange, the colonial administration promised monetary compensation after the war. The arrangement established the slave-backed loan system that would define the Sinchewese war economy: the colonial government issued state loans secured against the value of the enslaved labourers, and the landowners transferred their enslaved people to the military. The slave regiments raised through this system were deployed in the most dangerous sectors of the jungle front, and their mortality rate would far exceed that of regular Sinchewese troops over the course of the protracted land war.

=== 1712–1714: Protracted land war ===

From May 1712, the character of the war shifted from rapid Zong expansion to a protracted land campaign. The Volisanian colonial administration, having concentrated its forces at [[Sant Bart]], launched a counter-offensive aimed at recapturing Sant Cristòfor and the central isthmian territory. The counter-offensive was supported by Burawi naval forces under Admiral [[Xirsi Xaashi]], who raided Zong supply lines, and by Volisanian naval forces redeployed from the Aethiopean side of the isthmus. The naval redeployment was a lengthy process: the Volisanian Royal Navy forces stationed on the Aethiopean coast had to sail around the continent of [[Abyala]] to reach the Shendan Ocean, a voyage of approximately six months. The gradual arrival of these redeployed naval forces shifted the balance in Shendan waters over the course of 1712 and 1713, eroding the Zong naval dominance that had enabled the rapid capture of Sant Cristòfor.

The counter-offensive engaged the Zong expeditionary force along the [[Camí Ral]], the trans-isthmian road between [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the interior, where the character of the fighting differed markedly from the jungle campaign. The Volisanian colonial forces, under General [[Oriol March]], made extensive use of {{w|dragoon}}s and light cavalry for flanking and reconnaissance, leveraging their greater mobility in the open terrain to outmaneuver the Zong infantry. The Zong expeditionary force, under General [[Tan Peng-hui]] (陳鵬輝), was primarily infantry-based and relied on naval artillery from the fleet anchored in Sant Cristòfor harbour to support its defensive positions. The first major engagement of the counter-offensive was the [[Battle of Varakí]] in 1712, fought at [[Varakí]] along the Camí Ral on the shore of [[Lake Jarumá]]. The battle was the turning point of the field war: the Volisanian cavalry, supported by Burawi ground forces under General [[Petre Dhaxol]], halted the Zong expansion along the Camí Ral and pushed the Zong forces back to their encampments at [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]], on the inland outskirts of Sant Cristòfor. The battle demonstrated the vulnerability of infantry-heavy forces to cavalry flanking in open terrain and compelled the Zong command to contract its perimeter closer to Sant Cristòfor. In 1714, the Volisanian forces launched a second major assault on the Zong defensive perimeter at the [[Battle of la Riera]], named for the creek that crossed the battlefield south of Sant Cristòfor. The Zong forces, entrenched behind field fortifications and supported by naval artillery from the fleet, repelled repeated Volisanian cavalry and infantry assaults. The battle stabilised the Zong perimeter around Sant Cristòfor but did not enable a breakout, as the Volisanian forces retained the capacity to maintain the encirclement.

On land, the war expanded from the Free Sinchew campaign into a broader border conflict along the [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]]–[[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]] frontier, where Sinchewese ground forces clashed with Volisanian colonial troops and Balboan colonial militia. The land war was fought primarily by Sinchewese and Balboan forces, rather than by Zong mainland troops, and proved far more devastating than the concurrent naval operations. The land campaign advanced slowly, as the heavy jungle terrain of the southern isthmus and the resistance of the [[Free Sinchew]] army impeded conventional military movement. The border engagements were characterised by limited territorial gains on either side, as both forces were constrained by the difficult terrain and the limited logistical infrastructure of the frontier. The Volisanian colonial administration impressed Balboan slaves into military service, deploying them as labourers, porters, and front-line combatants in the border fighting. The use of enslaved Balboans in the war effort placed them in the most exposed and dangerous positions, and their casualties were disproportionately high. The slave regiments raised by the Sinchew colonial administration from October 1711, through the slave-backed loan system established earlier that year, continued to bear the brunt of the jungle fighting. Of the approximately 5,000 enslaved Sinchewese deployed in the war, an estimated 3,000 were killed, a mortality rate of approximately 60 percent.

The land war was further compounded by the rampant spread of disease. The isthmian jungle, already a hostile environment for conventional military operations, became a breeding ground for {{w|endemic disease|endemic}} and {{w|epidemic}} infections that afflicted both sides. Malaria, {{w|dysentery}}, and tropical fevers devastated the Sinchewese and Balboan forces, in many cases exceeding the casualties inflicted by combat. The disease environment was particularly lethal to troops drawn from non-tropical climates and to the enslaved Balboans, whose living conditions left them especially vulnerable.

The war also marked the first large-scale use in the Lurandian theater of new military technologies of the era. In the field battles near Sant Cristòfor, both sides deployed {{w|flintlock}} muskets, which had begun to replace the older {{w|matchlock}} mechanisms that had been in use since the previous century, and {{w|socket bayonet}}s, which allowed musketeers to function simultaneously as infantry and eliminated the need for separate pike formations. The open terrain of the coastal plains favoured conventional line tactics and cavalry operations, with the Volisanian forces making extensive use of dragoons and light cavalry for flanking and reconnaissance. In the isthmian jungle, the character of the fighting differed markedly. The dense vegetation rendered conventional line tactics impractical, and engagements were characterised by ambushes, skirmishes, and the difficulty of sustaining military columns in the tropical forest. The Free Sinchewese forces, supplied by Volisania, employed shortened {{w|carbine}}s and shorter-barrelled rifles suited to the confined spaces of the jungle, where the length of a standard musket was a hindrance. The Sinchewese colonial forces, initially equipped with standard flintlock muskets, suffered from the disadvantage of long barrels in the dense vegetation and sustained significant losses in the early engagements. As the jungle campaign progressed, the Sinchewese forces adapted by shortening the barrels of their muskets, producing improvised carbines better suited to the terrain. These technologies and adaptations increased the lethality of the land engagements and contributed to the high casualty rates.

Raids were conducted against the coast of [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] by Volisanian and Burawi naval forces seeking to disrupt Zong colonial supply lines. The Sinchew colonial garrison, under General [[Tan Tek-hui]] and Admiral [[Lim Chiau-seng]] (林昭成), defended the coastal settlements and supply depots against these raids. The campaign against [[Free Sinchew]] in the isthmian jungle, which had begun in August 1711, continued through 1712, 1713, and 1714, with the Sinchew colonial garrison gradually overwhelming the confederation's guerrilla strongholds, though the Free Sinchewese leadership remained at large.

=== 1715: Battle of Karawãtyba and the stalemate ===

In February 1715, the Sinchew colonial garrison under General [[Tan Tek-hui]] engaged the remaining Free Sinchewese forces at the [[Battle of Karawãtyba]], a decisive engagement fought at the principal Karawã settlement in the isthmian jungle. The battle resulted in the capture of the entire Free Sinchewese leadership, including Council Speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]] and the remaining members of the governing council. The Free Sinchewese forces, which had resisted the Sinchewese advance through guerrilla tactics and knowledge of the jungle terrain since 1711, were overwhelmed in the concentrated engagement. Following the military collapse of the confederation, the Zong colonial authorities issued the Free Sinchew Proclamation, an ultimatum that offered the Free Sinchewese population a choice between capitulation and annihilation. Those who accepted the proclamation were absorbed into the [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] colonial population; those who refused were systematically hunted down. The territory of Free Sinchew was reunified into the Sinchew colony, achieving one of the Zong court's two co-equal war objectives.

By 1715, the gradual redeployment of Volisanian naval forces from the Aethiopean side of the isthmus, which had begun after the fall of Sant Cristòfor in October 1711 and required the lengthy voyage around [[Abyala]], had reached sufficient strength in the Shendan theater to challenge the Zong naval position. Admiral [[Bernat de Vilanova]], now reinforced with the redeployed fleet elements, blockaded the Shendan approaches to [[Sant Cristòfor]] and surrounded the Zong expeditionary force on the [[Península de la Barcelona]] and the inner city walls of Sant Cristòfor. The Zong navy, though surrounded, maintained supply lines to the mainland and was able to sustain its position within the captured port. The Volisanian forces, unable to breach the Zong defences, conducted periodic raids against the Zong positions every few weeks but could not dislodge the garrison.

The result was a stalemate that persisted for approximately six months. The Zong forces resupplied Sant Cristòfor through their naval supply lines, while the Volisanian forces maintained the encirclement and continued their raids. Neither side could achieve a decisive breakthrough: the Zong could not break out of the encirclement, and the Volisanians could not penetrate the Zong defences. The prolonged stalemate, combined with the exhaustion of both coalitions, created pressure from both the Zong court and the Volisanian government to end the conflict.

=== 1716: Negotiations and treaty ===

With neither side able to achieve a military decision, negotiations opened directly between the commanders of the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]]'s colonial army and the Zong navy, who conducted the talks at Sant Cristòfor under pressure from their respective sovereigns to end the stalemate. The negotiations concluded on 12 January 1716 with the signing of the [[Treaty of Sant Cristòfor]]. The treaty was a compromise: Zong withdrew from Sant Cristòfor, returning the port to Volisanian control, but received the docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports that had been the original cause of the conflict. The [[Camí Ral]] remained under Volisanian administration, and the status quo ante borders between [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]] were restored.

== Theaters ==

The war was fought across three principal theaters, all located in or near the [[Isthmus of Balboa]] and the adjacent waters of the [[Shendan Ocean]].

=== Shendan Ocean theater ===

The primary theater of the war was the [[Shendan Ocean]] off the coast of [[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]], where the majority of naval operations took place. The Zong fleet, combining mainland and colonial vessels, initially held a decisive advantage in Shendan waters, as the bulk of the Volisanian Royal Navy was deployed on the Aethiopean side of the isthmus. This disparity enabled the Zong siege of [[Sant Cristòfor]] from April 1711 and the capture of the port in October 1711. The gradual redeployment of Volisanian naval forces from the Aethiopean coast, a process that required the lengthy voyage around [[Abyala]] and spanned from 1712 to 1715, eventually shifted the naval balance and enabled the Volisanian counter-blockade that isolated the Zong expeditionary force within the captured port.

=== Sinchew coastal raid theater ===

Volisanian and Burawi naval forces conducted raids against the coast of [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] beginning in 1712. The raids targeted coastal settlements, supply depots, and logistical infrastructure supporting the Zong expedition. The Sinchew colonial garrison, under General [[Tan Tek-hui]] and Admiral [[Lim Chiau-seng]], defended the coast and maintained the colony's supply lines despite the raids.

=== Sinchew–Balboa border land theater ===

The land theater was the most devastating of the war's three theaters and encompassed two distinct types of fighting. Along the Camí Ral between Sant Cristòfor and the interior, the war was fought as a conventional field campaign, with Volisanian dragoons and light cavalry engaging Zong infantry supported by naval artillery from the fleet in harbour. The [[Battle of Varakí]] (1712) and the [[Battle of la Riera]] (1714) were the principal engagements of the field war. In the isthmian jungle along the [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]]–[[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]] border, the fighting was characterised by ambushes, skirmishes, and guerrilla warfare, as Sinchewese ground forces clashed with Volisanian colonial troops, Balboan colonial militia, and [[Free Sinchew]]ese forces. The dense vegetation rendered conventional line tactics impractical, and both sides adapted by employing shortened carbines and shorter-barrelled rifles suited to the confined spaces. The border fighting was characterised by limited territorial gains, as both sides were constrained by the frontier terrain, the limited logistical infrastructure, and the rampant spread of disease. The border theater also encompassed the campaign against Free Sinchew in the isthmian jungle, where the Sinchew colonial garrison fought the confederation's guerrilla forces from August 1711 until the [[Battle of Karawãtyba]] and the conquest of Free Sinchew in February 1715. The land theater remained active from August 1711 until the armistice in January 1716.

== Maroon corsairs ==

[[Maroon pirates|Maroon corsairs]], operating as privateers rather than formal coalition members, harassed Zong shipping in the [[Shendan Ocean]] during the war. The most prominent of these corsair leaders was [[Don Ismael el-Nabulsi]], a commander of mixed Sephardic and [[Imfezzan peoples|Imfezzan]] ancestry, whose raids on Zong supply vessels disrupted the expeditionary force's logistics. The Maroon corsairs operated semi-independently, their activities coinciding with Volisanian interests but not conducted under formal Volisanian command. The corsairs' knowledge of the Shendan Ocean's coastal waters and their ability to strike Zong shipping from hidden bases made them a persistent threat to Zong supply lines throughout the conflict.

== Free Sinchew ==

{{main|Free Sinchew}}

[[Free Sinchew]] was a co-belligerent in the war, fighting alongside the Volisanian coalition though not under formal Volisanian command. The confederation's forces, numbering approximately 5,000 fighters, comprised Sinchewese holdouts from the [[Sinchew War]] and warriors of the [[Karawã People|Karawã]] and [[Apyãwa People|Apyãwa]] peoples. The Free Sinchewese forces were led by the confederation's governing council, with Council Speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]] (陳法生) serving as the principal military and political leader.

Free Sinchew's strategic position in the isthmian jungle between [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]] made it a co-equal objective of the Zong expedition. The confederation's control of the territory had frustrated Zong's earlier land invasion plans, and the reunification of Free Sinchew into the Sinchew colony was one of the two principal war aims pursued by the Zong court. In August 1711, the Sinchew colonial garrison under General [[Tan Tek-hui]] ordered the [[Sinchew Army Corps]] to advance through Free Sinchew territory, expecting the confederation to fold or permit passage. The Free Sinchewese forces instead attacked the advancing column, and approximately one week later dispatched correspondence to their Volisanian contacts requesting military assistance. Volisania, which had declared support for the Free Sinchewese Army in 1672, granted free passage for arms and began supplying heavy weaponry to the confederation. The Volisanian arms transformed the land campaign, enabling the Free Sinchewese forces to conduct sustained resistance against the Sinchew Army Corps. The Free Sinchewese forces, fighting on their home terrain, relied on guerrilla tactics, ambushes, and knowledge of the jungle's paths and waterways to resist the Sinchew colonial garrison's advance from August 1711.

The campaign concluded in February 1715 at the [[Battle of Karawãtyba]], where the Sinchew colonial garrison under General [[Tan Tek-hui]] engaged and defeated the remaining Free Sinchewese forces at the principal Karawã settlement in the isthmian jungle. The entire Free Sinchewese leadership, including Council Speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]], was captured in the battle. The confederation's forces suffered approximately 2,000 killed in the campaign. Following the military collapse, the Zong colonial authorities issued the Free Sinchew Proclamation, an ultimatum that offered the Free Sinchewese population a choice between capitulation and annihilation. Those who capitulated were absorbed into the Sinchew colonial population; those who refused were killed or captured. The entire leadership of Free Sinchew, including Council Speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]], was tried and executed. The [[Karawã People|Karawã]] and [[Apyãwa People|Apyãwa]] communities were dispersed, with most members killed or captured; those who survived were absorbed into the Sinchew colonial population, losing their distinct political identity.

== Treaty of Sant Cristòfor ==

{{main|Treaty of Sant Cristòfor}}

The [[Treaty of Sant Cristòfor]], signed on 12 January 1716 at [[Sant Cristòfor]], ended the First War of the Isthmus. The treaty was negotiated directly between the commanders of the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]]'s colonial army and the Zong navy, who conducted the talks at the besieged port under pressure from their respective sovereigns to end the prolonged stalemate. The treaty's principal terms were:

* Zong withdrawal from [[Sant Cristòfor]], returning the port to Volisanian control
* Zong granted docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports, providing the commercial access that had been the original cause of the conflict
* The [[Camí Ral]] remained under Volisanian administration
* The isthmian corridor was established as a matter of international concern, establishing the principle that trans-isthmian trade was not solely a Volisanian domestic matter
* [[Sant Cristòfor]] was designated a [[free port]], open to commerce from all nations
* The status quo ante borders between [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] and the [[Viceroyalty of Balboa]] were restored
* The reunification of [[Free Sinchew]] into [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]], accomplished by military conquest during the war, was recognised

The treaty was a compromise that satisfied neither side fully. Zong had failed to hold Sant Cristòfor but had obtained the commercial access it sought and had achieved the reunification of Free Sinchew. Volisania had defended its territorial claims but had been forced to grant the very concessions the war was fought to prevent and had lost a co-belligerent that had served as a buffer in the isthmian jungle.

== Aftermath ==

=== Volisania ===

The Treaty of Sant Cristòfor was regarded as a humiliation in Volisania. The Volisanian state had mobilised vast resources — naval forces, marine corps, colonial garrisons, and the military commitments of its allies — to defend Sant Cristòfor, only to grant Zong the docking and passage rights that the war had been fought to deny. The loss of Free Sinchew, which Volisania had supported since its 1672 declaration, further compounded the humiliation: the co-belligerent that had served as a buffer in the isthmian jungle was conquered and dissolved, leaving Volisania without a proxy presence in the isthmian interior. The treaty's outcome undermined the credibility of the Volisanian colonial administration and contributed to the political instability that culminated in the [[Volisanian Civil War]] of 1736, in which the [[House of Domènech]] was deposed and the colonial governance apparatus collapsed.

=== Burawa ===

Burawa's participation in the war was costly in both blood and treasure. The kingdom had committed substantial naval forces under Admiral [[Xirsi Xaashi]] and ground forces under General [[Petre Dhaxol]] at the behest of its strategic alignment with Volisania, but gained nothing from the peace. The war's outcome called into question the value of the Volisanian alliance, a theme that would recur in Burawi politics throughout the [[Four House Era]]. King [[Iacob V of Burawa|Iacob V]], who had committed Burawi forces to the war, faced increasing domestic criticism over the alliance's costs and the absence of tangible gains.

=== Zong ===

[[Tianzheng of Zong|Emperor Tianzheng]], who had authorised the military preparations at Tamsui, died in 1711 before word of the attack on Sant Cristòfor reached the Zong homeland. His successor, [[Tianli of Zong|Emperor Tianli]], inherited a war that had been initiated by the naval command without prior court approval. Tianli, who had been regarded as a meek replacement for his father, seized upon the news of the Barcelona peninsula capture as an opportunity to prove himself a strong leader. The [[Zong Societal Assembly|Societal Assembly]], which shared the court's imperialist disposition and regarded Volisania as a strategic threat, enthusiastically endorsed the continuation and expansion of the war effort. Although Zong had failed to capture and hold Sant Cristòfor, the treaty delivered the commercial access to Volisanian ports that had been the underlying objective of the war. Zong merchants gained docking and passage rights through Volisanian ports, and the designation of Sant Cristòfor as a free port further opened the trans-isthmian corridor to Zong commerce. The reunification of Free Sinchew into Sinchew, accomplished by military conquest during the war, achieved the second of the court's co-equal objectives and restored Zong colonial authority over the isthmian jungle. The war's outcome was thus a strategic success for Zong, albeit one achieved at significant cost.

In 1718, Emperor Tianli issued an edict banning slavery in all Zong lands, including the colonies of Sinchew and Louyang. The edict formalised the principle, long maintained by the Zong court with respect to the mainland, that enslaved people did not exist as legal entities. The [[Secret Trade]] — the system by which enslaved Dagit and Marosian peoples had been brought to Sinchew since the late 16th century — had operated in legal ambiguity under the [[Zong Edict of 1500]], which banned slavery on the mainland but did not explicitly address the colonies. The 1718 edict resolved the ambiguity by extending the prohibition to all Zong territories. The court refused to recognise the slave-backed loans that the Sinchewese colonial administration had taken from landowners during the war, on the grounds that the legal basis for the loans — the existence of enslaved people as property — had never been valid.

=== Sinchew ===

The war's impact on Sinchew was catastrophic. The colony had raised large slave regiments for the land war through the slave-backed loan system established in October 1711, in which the colonial government issued state loans secured against the value of enslaved labourers contributed by landowners. Of the approximately 5,000 enslaved Sinchewese deployed in the war, an estimated 3,000 were killed — a mortality rate of approximately 60 percent, far exceeding that of regular Sinchewese troops. The landowners, who had expected the return of their enslaved labourers or compensation for their loss, demanded payment from the Zong court after the war.

The Zong court refused to compensate the landowners, maintaining the position that enslaved people did not legally exist under the Edict of 1500 and that the loans were therefore void. The refusal to acknowledge the slave-backed loans wiped out the investments of the Sinchewese landowning class, who had lent their enslaved labourers to the war effort in expectation of compensation or return. In 1717, the landowners mounted a rebellion against the colonial administration in response to the court's refusal. The rebellion was suppressed, but the political fracture it revealed would deepen in subsequent years.

The 1718 emancipation edict forced Sinchew to write laws abolishing all slavery within the colony, freeing the remaining enslaved population without compensation to their former owners. The abrupt emancipation caused an economic crash in Sinchew, as the plantation economy, dependent on enslaved labour, collapsed. The Zong court provided no financial assistance to the colony, regarding the emancipation as a legal necessity consistent with the Edict of 1500 rather than a matter for compensation.

The economic devastation and the Zong court's refusal to assist created a deep resentment among the Sinchewese population that transcended the landowning class. The colony's marginalisation within the Zong commonwealth, combined with the economic collapse and the political repression following the 1717 rebellion, contributed to the conditions that led to the [[Sinchewese Revolutionary War]] (1726–1733), in which Sinchew achieved independence from Zong and established the modern Sinchewese state.

=== Free Sinchew ===

The conquest and reunification of [[Free Sinchew]] into the [[Sinchew Colony|Sinchew]] colony ended over half a century of independent governance by the confederation of Sinchewese holdouts and indigenous peoples. Following the military collapse of the confederation at the [[Battle of Karawãtyba]] in February 1715, the Zong colonial authorities issued the Free Sinchew Proclamation, an ultimatum that offered the Free Sinchewese population a choice between capitulation and annihilation. The entire leadership of Free Sinchew, including Council Speaker [[Tân Huat-seng]], was tried and executed. The [[Karawã People|Karawã]] and [[Apyãwa People|Apyãwa]] communities were dispersed, with most members killed or captured; those who survived the proclamation were absorbed into the Sinchew colonial population and lost their distinct political identity. The dissolution of Free Sinchew removed the buffer that had separated the Zong and Volisanian colonial spheres in the isthmian interior, creating a direct Sinchew–Balboa land border that would become a source of continued tension in subsequent decades.

=== Guajicutea ===

The war created enormous demand for ships, naval stores, and repair facilities, from which the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]] in [[Guajicutea]] benefited. The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] was expanded to support the Volisanian war effort. After the war, however, the opening of trade routes to Zong territories led to an influx of cheap Zong manufactured goods into the Capuyaquiran market. The flood of Zong cotton products, in particular, undercut local production and effectively destroyed Guajicutea's cotton industry, forcing the colony to shift to [[sugar]], [[tobacco]], and tropical [[dye]]s as replacement cash crops.

=== Sant Cristòfor ===

The designation of [[Sant Cristòfor]] as a free port transformed the city. No longer solely a Volisanian military outpost, Sant Cristòfor developed as a cosmopolitan entrepôt where Zong, Illypnian, and Abyalan merchants conducted business alongside one another. The city's commercial prosperity contrasted with the economic stagnation of the Balboan interior, and its cosmopolitan character became a defining feature of its identity in subsequent centuries.

